em","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deliverApproval"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sweep"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime does not require one process to own a run forever. Each call claims a\nrun, drives it until it completes or pauses, releases the lease, and returns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"execution-store","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Execution store"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime depends on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowExecutionStore"},{"type":"text","value":". It is richer than the core\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" because serverless and multi-worker deployments need more than replay:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"idempotent run creation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"append-only event logs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run state and pause metadata"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"timers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"signal and approval deliveries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"schedule definitions and schedule buckets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"atomic claiming and leases"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stale run recovery"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list and timeline APIs"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The store is the durability boundary. If a host execution exits, another drive\ncan resume because the store knows the run state, event log, timers, and pending\nwaits."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"host-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host adapters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host adapters are thin. They do not define workflow semantics. They adapt a\ndeployment provider's entrypoint to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Netlify:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both adapters:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"materialize registered workflow schedules"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runtime.sweep"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeEvents: false"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"return a compact summary response"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-a-sweep-does","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What a sweep does"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sweep is a bounded unit of background work:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.sweep({\n  maxRecoveredRuns: 25,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claims expired running executions and replays them from checkpoints."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claims due schedule buckets and starts those workflow runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claims due timers and delivers "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__timer"},{"type":"text","value":" signals to sleeping runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stops when it reaches count or time budgets."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The response includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"summary"},{"type":"text","value":": counts by result kind and event counts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deadlineReached"},{"type":"text","value":": true if "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDurationMs"},{"type":"text","value":" stopped the sweep"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"remainingMayExist"},{"type":"text","value":": true if another sweep may be useful"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the safety valve for serverless hosts. A sweep should be small enough to\nfit comfortably inside one host execution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"leases","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leases"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leases prevent two workers from executing the same run or due timer at the same\ntime. They are intentionally time-bounded:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a worker claims work with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leaseOwner"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leaseMs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the runtime renews the run lease every third of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leaseMs"},{"type":"text","value":" while driving"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"another worker cannot claim it until the lease expires"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"if the worker crashes, stale lease recovery can claim it later"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leases are not a correctness substitute for idempotency. External side effects\nstill need idempotency keys, and signal deliveries still need stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signalId"},{"type":"text","value":"\nvalues."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"code-loading-and-versioning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code loading and versioning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow registrations use async loaders:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"workflows: {\n  fulfillment: {\n    load: () => import('./fulfillment').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentWorkflow),\n    version: 'v2',\n    previousVersions: {\n      v1: () => import('./fulfillment.v1').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentV1),\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This shape helps with:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"code splitting"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"lazy loading"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keeping old workflow versions available for paused runs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"loading workflow code outside a closed monorepo TypeScript loop"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The store persists stable identifiers like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflowId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflowVersion"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". It does not persist function closures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"memory-and-event-collection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory and event collection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct calls like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startRun"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deliverSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" return emitted events by\ndefault because they are often used by HTTP handlers or tests."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sweeps should usually avoid retaining events:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.sweep({ includeEvents: false })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime still counts all events in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eventCount"},{"type":"text","value":"; it just does not keep the\nfull event array in memory. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" if you want a small sample:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.sweep({\n  includeEvents: true,\n  maxEvents: 100,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This keeps busy cron sweeps from exploding memory while still making summaries\nobservable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"publish"},{"type":"text","value":" for live fan-out without retaining events in the result:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"await runtime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId,\n  input,\n  includeEvents: false,\n  publish: (runId, event) => eventBus.publish(runId, event),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Publishers are best-effort and do not participate in durable execution."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-remains-user-owned","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What remains user-owned"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Workflow does not hide infrastructure choices:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"choose the store adapter"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"choose cron cadence"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"choose queue or webhook ingress"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"choose retention policies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"choose dashboards and alerting"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapters make the default path easier, but the runtime stays headless."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"runtime-model","text":"Runtime model","level":1},{"id":"core-engine","text":"Core engine","level":2},{"id":"runtime","text":"Runtime","level":2},{"id":"execution-store","text":"Execution store","level":2},{"id":"host-adapters","text":"Host adapters","level":2},{"id":"what-a-sweep-does","text":"What a sweep does","level":2},{"id":"leases","text":"Leases","level":2},{"id":"code-loading-and-versioning","text":"Code loading and versioning","level":2},{"id":"memory-and-event-collection","text":"Memory and event collection","level":2},{"id":"what-remains-user-owned","text":"What remains user-owned","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# Runtime model\n\nTanStack Workflow has three layers:\n\n1. **Core engine**: deterministic replay and workflow primitives.\n2. **Runtime**: execution ownership, schedules, timers, signals, approvals, and\n   leases.\n3. **Adapters**: stores and host entrypoints for a specific database or\n   deployment environment.\n\nThat split keeps the core headless while still giving production apps the\noperational pieces they need.\n\n## Core engine\n\n`@tanstack/workflow-core` is the small replay engine. It owns:\n\n- `createWorkflow`\n- `ctx.step`\n- `ctx.waitForEvent`\n- `ctx.approve`\n- `ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil`\n- `ctx.now` and `ctx.uuid`\n- middleware\n- version routing through `version` and `previousVersions`\n- the low-level `RunStore` used by `runWorkflow`\n\nThe core engine is intentionally not a scheduler, queue, database adapter, or\ndeployment adapter.\n\n## Runtime\n\n`@tanstack/workflow-runtime` wraps the core engine with a production execution\nmodel:\n\n```ts\nconst runtime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows: {\n    fulfillment: {\n      load: async () => fulfillmentWorkflow,\n      schedules: [\n        {\n          id: 'fulfillment-every-15m',\n          schedule: every.minutes(15),\n          overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n          input: { batchSize: 100 },\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe runtime exposes:\n\n- `startRun`\n- `deliverSignal`\n- `deliverApproval`\n- `sweep`\n\nThe runtime does not require one process to own a run forever. Each call claims a\nrun, drives it until it completes or pauses, releases the lease, and returns.\n\n## Execution store\n\nThe runtime depends on `WorkflowExecutionStore`. It is richer than the core\n`RunStore` because serverless and multi-worker deployments need more than replay:\n\n- idempotent run creation\n- append-only event logs\n- run state and pause metadata\n- timers\n- signal and approval deliveries\n- schedule definitions and schedule buckets\n- atomic claiming and leases\n- stale run recovery\n- list and timeline APIs\n\nThe store is the durability boundary. If a host execution exits, another drive\ncan resume because the store knows the run state, event log, timers, and pending\nwaits.\n\n## Host adapters\n\nHost adapters are thin. They do not define workflow semantics. They adapt a\ndeployment provider's entrypoint to `runtime.sweep()`.\n\nNetlify:\n\n```ts\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n```\n\nVercel:\n\n```ts\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nBoth adapters:\n\n- materialize registered workflow schedules\n- call `runtime.sweep`\n- default to `includeEvents: false`\n- return a compact summary response\n\n## What a sweep does\n\nA sweep is a bounded unit of background work:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxRecoveredRuns: 25,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe runtime:\n\n1. Claims expired running executions and replays them from checkpoints.\n2. Claims due schedule buckets and starts those workflow runs.\n3. Claims due timers and delivers `__timer` signals to sleeping runs.\n4. Stops when it reaches count or time budgets.\n\nThe response includes:\n\n- `summary`: counts by result kind and event counts\n- `deadlineReached`: true if `maxDurationMs` stopped the sweep\n- `remainingMayExist`: true if another sweep may be useful\n\nThis is the safety valve for serverless hosts. A sweep should be small enough to\nfit comfortably inside one host execution.\n\n## Leases\n\nLeases prevent two workers from executing the same run or due timer at the same\ntime. They are intentionally time-bounded:\n\n- a worker claims work with `leaseOwner` and `leaseMs`\n- the runtime renews the run lease every third of `leaseMs` while driving\n- another worker cannot claim it until the lease expires\n- if the worker crashes, stale lease recovery can claim it later\n\nLeases are not a correctness substitute for idempotency. External side effects\nstill need idempotency keys, and signal deliveries still need stable `signalId`\nvalues.\n\n## Code loading and versioning\n\nWorkflow registrations use async loaders:\n\n```ts\nworkflows: {\n  fulfillment: {\n    load: () => import('./fulfillment').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentWorkflow),\n    version: 'v2',\n    previousVersions: {\n      v1: () => import('./fulfillment.v1').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentV1),\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThis shape helps with:\n\n- code splitting\n- lazy loading\n- keeping old workflow versions available for paused runs\n- loading workflow code outside a closed monorepo TypeScript loop\n\nThe store persists stable identifiers like `workflowId`, `workflowVersion`, and\n`runId`. It does not persist function closures.\n\n## Memory and event collection\n\nDirect calls like `startRun` and `deliverSignal` return emitted events by\ndefault because they are often used by HTTP handlers or tests.\n\nSweeps should usually avoid retaining events:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({ includeEvents: false })\n```\n\nThe runtime still counts all events in `eventCount`; it just does not keep the\nfull event array in memory. Use `maxEvents` if you want a small sample:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  includeEvents: true,\n  maxEvents: 100,\n})\n```\n\nThis keeps busy cron sweeps from exploding memory while still making summaries\nobservable.\n\nUse `publish` for live fan-out without retaining events in the result:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId,\n  input,\n  includeEvents: false,\n  publish: (runId, event) => eventBus.publish(runId, event),\n})\n```\n\nPublishers are best-effort and do not participate in durable execution.\n\n## What remains user-owned\n\nTanStack Workflow does not hide infrastructure choices:\n\n- choose the store adapter\n- choose cron cadence\n- choose queue or webhook ingress\n- choose retention policies\n- choose dashboards and alerting\n\nThe adapters make the default path easier, but the runtime stays headless.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: runtime-model\ntitle: Runtime Model\n---\n\n# Runtime model\n\nTanStack Workflow has three layers:\n\n1. **Core engine**: deterministic replay and workflow primitives.\n2. **Runtime**: execution ownership, schedules, timers, signals, approvals, and\n   leases.\n3. **Adapters**: stores and host entrypoints for a specific database or\n   deployment environment.\n\nThat split keeps the core headless while still giving production apps the\noperational pieces they need.\n\n## Core engine\n\n`@tanstack/workflow-core` is the small replay engine. It owns:\n\n- `createWorkflow`\n- `ctx.step`\n- `ctx.waitForEvent`\n- `ctx.approve`\n- `ctx.sleep` and `ctx.sleepUntil`\n- `ctx.now` and `ctx.uuid`\n- middleware\n- version routing through `version` and `previousVersions`\n- the low-level `RunStore` used by `runWorkflow`\n\nThe core engine is intentionally not a scheduler, queue, database adapter, or\ndeployment adapter.\n\n## Runtime\n\n`@tanstack/workflow-runtime` wraps the core engine with a production execution\nmodel:\n\n```ts\nconst runtime = defineWorkflowRuntime({\n  store,\n  workflows: {\n    fulfillment: {\n      load: async () => fulfillmentWorkflow,\n      schedules: [\n        {\n          id: 'fulfillment-every-15m',\n          schedule: every.minutes(15),\n          overlapPolicy: 'skip',\n          input: { batchSize: 100 },\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe runtime exposes:\n\n- `startRun`\n- `deliverSignal`\n- `deliverApproval`\n- `sweep`\n\nThe runtime does not require one process to own a run forever. Each call claims a\nrun, drives it until it completes or pauses, releases the lease, and returns.\n\n## Execution store\n\nThe runtime depends on `WorkflowExecutionStore`. It is richer than the core\n`RunStore` because serverless and multi-worker deployments need more than replay:\n\n- idempotent run creation\n- append-only event logs\n- run state and pause metadata\n- timers\n- signal and approval deliveries\n- schedule definitions and schedule buckets\n- atomic claiming and leases\n- stale run recovery\n- list and timeline APIs\n\nThe store is the durability boundary. If a host execution exits, another drive\ncan resume because the store knows the run state, event log, timers, and pending\nwaits.\n\n## Host adapters\n\nHost adapters are thin. They do not define workflow semantics. They adapt a\ndeployment provider's entrypoint to `runtime.sweep()`.\n\nNetlify:\n\n```ts\nexport default createNetlifyWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  maxDurationMs: 25_000,\n})\n```\n\nVercel:\n\n```ts\nexport const GET = createVercelWorkflowSweepHandler({\n  runtime: workflowRuntime,\n  cronSecret: process.env.CRON_SECRET,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n})\n```\n\nBoth adapters:\n\n- materialize registered workflow schedules\n- call `runtime.sweep`\n- default to `includeEvents: false`\n- return a compact summary response\n\n## What a sweep does\n\nA sweep is a bounded unit of background work:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  maxRecoveredRuns: 25,\n  maxScheduledRuns: 25,\n  maxTimers: 25,\n  maxDurationMs: 55_000,\n  includeEvents: false,\n})\n```\n\nThe runtime:\n\n1. Claims expired running executions and replays them from checkpoints.\n2. Claims due schedule buckets and starts those workflow runs.\n3. Claims due timers and delivers `__timer` signals to sleeping runs.\n4. Stops when it reaches count or time budgets.\n\nThe response includes:\n\n- `summary`: counts by result kind and event counts\n- `deadlineReached`: true if `maxDurationMs` stopped the sweep\n- `remainingMayExist`: true if another sweep may be useful\n\nThis is the safety valve for serverless hosts. A sweep should be small enough to\nfit comfortably inside one host execution.\n\n## Leases\n\nLeases prevent two workers from executing the same run or due timer at the same\ntime. They are intentionally time-bounded:\n\n- a worker claims work with `leaseOwner` and `leaseMs`\n- the runtime renews the run lease every third of `leaseMs` while driving\n- another worker cannot claim it until the lease expires\n- if the worker crashes, stale lease recovery can claim it later\n\nLeases are not a correctness substitute for idempotency. External side effects\nstill need idempotency keys, and signal deliveries still need stable `signalId`\nvalues.\n\n## Code loading and versioning\n\nWorkflow registrations use async loaders:\n\n```ts\nworkflows: {\n  fulfillment: {\n    load: () => import('./fulfillment').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentWorkflow),\n    version: 'v2',\n    previousVersions: {\n      v1: () => import('./fulfillment.v1').then((mod) => mod.fulfillmentV1),\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThis shape helps with:\n\n- code splitting\n- lazy loading\n- keeping old workflow versions available for paused runs\n- loading workflow code outside a closed monorepo TypeScript loop\n\nThe store persists stable identifiers like `workflowId`, `workflowVersion`, and\n`runId`. It does not persist function closures.\n\n## Memory and event collection\n\nDirect calls like `startRun` and `deliverSignal` return emitted events by\ndefault because they are often used by HTTP handlers or tests.\n\nSweeps should usually avoid retaining events:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({ includeEvents: false })\n```\n\nThe runtime still counts all events in `eventCount`; it just does not keep the\nfull event array in memory. Use `maxEvents` if you want a small sample:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.sweep({\n  includeEvents: true,\n  maxEvents: 100,\n})\n```\n\nThis keeps busy cron sweeps from exploding memory while still making summaries\nobservable.\n\nUse `publish` for live fan-out without retaining events in the result:\n\n```ts\nawait runtime.startRun({\n  workflowId: 'fulfillment',\n  runId,\n  input,\n  includeEvents: false,\n  publish: (runId, event) => eventBus.publish(runId, event),\n})\n```\n\nPublishers are best-effort and do not participate in durable execution.\n\n## What remains user-owned\n\nTanStack Workflow does not hide infrastructure choices:\n\n- choose the store adapter\n- choose cron cadence\n- choose queue or webhook ingress\n- choose retention policies\n- choose dashboards and alerting\n\nThe adapters make the default path easier, but the runtime stays headless.\n"}
